---

<section id="philosophy" class="py-24 bg-slate-900 text-white">
  <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6">
    <h2 class="text-xs font-semibold text-teal tracking-widest uppercase mb-8">Leadership Philosophy</h2>
    <h3 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold mb-10 leading-tight">
      Curiosity is the job.
    </h3>
    <div class="max-w-2xl space-y-6 text-lg text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
      <p>
        Most descriptions of good support engineering focus on being thorough, following process,
        escalating at the right time. Those things matter. But the real signal is curiosity.
        A curious engineer doesn't just solve the problem in front of them — they follow the thread.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-14 grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-8">
      <div class="bg-slate-800/60 rounded-xl p-8 border border-slate-700/50">
        <div class="text-teal text-2xl font-bold mb-4">01</div>
        <h4 class="text-white font-semibold text-lg mb-3">The edge case</h4>
        <p class="text-slate-400 leading-relaxed">
          A documented issue comes in. A methodical engineer sends the doc and closes the ticket.
          A curious engineer notices something slightly off — the error is a little different, the
          timing doesn't quite fit — so they send the doc <em class="text-slate-300">and</em> ask
          for logs anyway. They find an edge case engineering has never seen. One moment of
          noticing, completely different outcome.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="bg-slate-800/60 rounded-xl p-8 border border-slate-700/50">
        <div class="text-teal text-2xl font-bold mb-4">02</div>
        <h4 class="text-white font-semibold text-lg mb-3">The bug that becomes a KB</h4>
        <p class="text-slate-400 leading-relaxed">
          An engineer finds a bug. They could write it up with logs and a code pointer, send it
          to engineering, and move on. A curious engineer notices two services being touched that
          they don't fully understand — so they dig in. They read the code. They figure out what
          those services should and shouldn't do. They come back with an internal KB article and
          lead a lunch-and-learn. That's what high-performing support engineers do.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-14 pt-10 border-t border-slate-800">
      <h4 class="text-slate-400 text-xs font-semibold tracking-widest uppercase mb-8">
        What I believe
      </h4>
      <ul class="space-y-5 max-w-2xl">
        <li class="flex gap-4">
          <span class="text-teal mt-1 flex-shrink-0">—</span>
          <p class="text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
            <strong class="text-white">Technical depth is non-negotiable.</strong> My engineers
            read code, run reproductions, and trace logs. They don't just relay symptoms.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-4">
          <span class="text-teal mt-1 flex-shrink-0">—</span>
          <p class="text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
            <strong class="text-white">White-glove support is a product feature, not a service tier.</strong>
            Dedicated engineers and Slack-based support require a different caliber of person and
            a different way of building a team.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-4">
          <span class="text-teal mt-1 flex-shrink-0">—</span>
          <p class="text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
            <strong class="text-white">The feedback loop is the job.</strong> Customer pain should
            become documented edge cases should become engineering priorities. Most support orgs
            break that loop. I work to close it.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-4">
          <span class="text-teal mt-1 flex-shrink-0">—</span>
          <p class="text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
            <strong class="text-white">Player/coach leadership.</strong> At director level, I stay
            close enough to the work to know when a problem is genuinely hard versus just unclear.
            There's a difference, and it matters for how you lead.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
